  SECURITY REPORT — Score: 70/100 [MEDIUM]
  The codebase has several notable security concerns: public seed endpoint without authentication, hard‑coded demo passwords, overly permissive CORS settings, and missing input length validation. While password hashing is correctly implemented and ORM usage mitigates SQL injection, adding authentication, tightening CORS, enforcing field length limits, and applying rate limiting will substantially improve the security posture.

  Findings (6):
  [HIGH    ] AuthenticationGap in app/routes/seed_routes.py
             The `/seed` endpoint is publicly accessible and does not require any authentication or authorization, allowing anyone to populate or modify the database.
             Fix: Add `Depends(get_current_user)` (or a dedicated admin check) to the endpoint and restrict it to admin/author roles only.
  [HIGH    ] HardcodedSecret in app/routes/seed_routes.py
             Demo users are created with a hard‑coded password (`Password123`). This password is stored in source code and could be discovered in version control.
             Fix: Replace hard‑coded passwords with randomly generated ones or require the seed script to accept passwords via environment variables. Do not commit any plaintext passwords.
  [MEDIUM  ] CORSIssue in app/main.py
             CORS is configured to allow any origin (`"*"`), which is unsafe for production as it permits any website to make requests to the API.
             Fix: Restrict `allow_origins` to the list of trusted front‑end domains, e.g., `allow_origins=["https://myapp.com"]`.
  [LOW     ] MissingValidation in app/schemas/post.py
             String fields such as `title`, `content_markdown`, `cover_image_url`, and `name` have only a minimum length constraint and no maximum length, allowing excessively large payloads.
             Fix: Add `max_length` constraints appropriate for each field, e.g., `Field(min_length=1, max_length=255)` for title.
  [LOW     ] MissingValidation in app/schemas/auth.py
             The `display_name` field lacks a maximum length limit, which could be abused for DoS attacks.
             Fix: Add a reasonable `max_length`, e.g., `Field(..., min_length=1, max_length=100)`.
  [INFO    ] RateLimitingAbsent in app/routes/auth_routes.py
             Login and registration endpoints do not implement any rate‑limiting, making them vulnerable to credential‑stuffing and brute‑force attacks.
             Fix: Integrate a rate‑limiting solution (e.g., slowapi, Redis‑based limiter) on authentication routes.

  Passed: No raw SQL string concatenation found (SQLAlchemy ORM used), Passwords are hashed using a secure algorithm (bcrypt) before storage
